	import Closeups from './overview/Closeups.svelte';
	import IconsSample from './overview/IconsSample.svelte';
	import Posters from './overview/Posters.svelte';

	const { axis, glyphCount, styles } = specimen;
	const stems = styles.map((s) => s.stem);
</script>

<Hero kicker="Two variable, open-source fonts for llama.cpp" tagline={['One set of outlines.', 'Two versions.', `Every weight from ${axis.min} to ${axis.max}.`]} />

<Section label="The sans of the Llama family" columns>

A neo-grotesk with an even stroke, round bowls and open, angle-cut terminals. Llama Sans comes in two variable fonts that share their outlines, with {glyphCount} glyphs each and the Llama icon built in at U+E000. Mono and Serif will follow.

UI is for interfaces and running text: spaced and kerned like the system fonts at their text sizes, on a line box centred on the cap height, with slightly deeper descenders. Display is for headlines: tighter, with firmer kerning and compact lines.

</Section>

<Section label="Clear glass">

Beatrice Warde compared good type to a crystal goblet: clear enough that you see the wine, not the glass. Llama Sans aims for the same in an interface. Even strokes, calm round bowls and an even rhythm keep it out of the way of labels, messages and long reading alike, so it can carry a whole product without asking for attention.

It isn't featureless, though. A few angled cuts give it just enough character to lead the eye along the line, and they come forward only when you look for them.

<Closeups />

</Section>

