<Section label="Design notes">
	<Notes>
		<Note term="Stroke">Near-monoline, with a {stems[0]}-unit stem at 400 rising to {stems.at(-1)} at 700. Where a bowl, arm or shoulder leaves a stem, the notch cuts a little deeper into the joint, so joins stay open without turning into ink traps.</Note>
		<Note term="Curves">Bowls are eased toward true circles, fully circular in G, S and s, so rounds read soft and calm without turning geometric.</Note>
		<Note term="Terminals">Every curved stroke end is trimmed the same short distance and cut almost square to the stroke, which keeps c, e, g, s, 2, 3, 5, 6 and 9 open and consistent.</Note>
		<Note term="a">Double-story, on a straight stem with a flat foot. The hook runs long and ends in a flat cut; the bowl rises gently into the stem with sharp joints. Toward Bold, a small bracketed foot appears.</Note>
		<Note term="l, r, t, f">A plain l the width of the i, and a narrow t with short arms. The ends of r, t and f are angled to match the curved terminals.</Note>
		<Note term="G, S, s">G and S wide and round, s a little leaner, with slightly open, angled ends on S and s.</Note>
		<Note term="Weights">Regular, Medium, SemiBold and Bold at 400, 500, 600 and 700, a little lighter than the numbers usually mean, with every weight in between.</Note>
	</Notes>
</Section>

<Section label="Next pass">

A straight-legged R and a shorter Q tail, an angled end on the j, slightly wider o, c, e and O, and a review of the accented glyphs.

</Section>

